## 19. Classes & OOP

A class bundles data (attributes) and behavior (methods) together. `__init__` sets up a new instance; `self` refers to the specific instance a method is called on. Double-underscore attributes (`self.__name`) are name-mangled to discourage direct outside access, which is why getter/setter methods are provided.

### A class with private attributes and getters/setters

```python
class Pet:
    def __init__(self, type, age, name):
        self.__type = type
        self.__age = age
        self.__name = name

    def set_type(self, type):
        self.__type = type
    def set_age(self, age):
        self.__age = age
    def set_name(self, name):
        self.__name = name
    
    def get_type(self):
        return self.__type
    def get_age(self):
        return self.__age
    def get_name(self):
        return self.__name
    
    def __str__(self):
        return ('Name: ' + str(self.__name)
                + '\nType: ' + str(self.__type)
                + '\nAge: ' + str(self.__age))
```

